diff --git a/docs/OPERATOR-GUIDE.md b/docs/OPERATOR-GUIDE.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..ea2bc2e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/OPERATOR-GUIDE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,292 @@
+# Operator guide
+
+How to use the factory, start to finish. An operator starts projects, writes
+work orders, runs the line and hands the result to a human for the merge.
+
+For credentials, access control and repository settings see
+[OPERATOR-REQUIREMENTS.md](OPERATOR-REQUIREMENTS.md). For how the runtime
+works see [EXECUTION.md](EXECUTION.md).
+
+## The short version
+
+```bash
+# 1. Scaffold a project next to the factory
+python3 -m anvil.cli new-project ../my-project --name MyProject --blueprint python-service --idea "What this project should prove"
+
+# 2. In the project: link the factory, add the runtime block to anvil.yaml (step 2 below), write a work order
+cd ../my-project
+ln -s ../anvil-factory .anvil-factory
+
+# 3. Dry run: no model calls, shows what each station will be asked
+PYTHONPATH=.anvil-factory python3 -m anvil.cli run . --pipeline .anvil-factory/pipelines/plan-build-test.json --work-order work-orders/wo-1.json
+
+# 4. Real run, on a run branch
+git checkout -b anvil/run-wo-1
+PYTHONPATH=.anvil-factory python3 -m anvil.cli run . --pipeline .anvil-factory/pipelines/plan-build-test.json --work-order work-orders/wo-1.json --execute
+
+# 5. Read runs/wo-1/report.md, commit, push the branch, open a pull request. A human merges.
+```
+
+## 1. Before you start
+
+You need, once:
+
+| Need | Check |
+|---|---|
+| Git and Python 3.11 or later | `python3 --version` |
+| GitHub CLI, signed in | `gh auth status` |
+| A station runtime, one of the two below | the checks in each row |
+
+| Runtime | `ANVIL_RUNTIME` | What it needs |
+|---|---|---|
+| Claude Code and Codex through the gateway | unset, or `claude-codex` (default) | The `claude` and `codex` CLIs on PATH, plus the gateway environment: `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, and `CF_AIG_TOKEN` or the `cf-aig-authorization` header in `ANTHROPIC_CUSTOM_HEADERS`. See [OPERATOR-REQUIREMENTS.md](OPERATOR-REQUIREMENTS.md) |
+| GitHub Copilot CLI | `copilot` | `npm install -g @github/copilot` (Node 22 or later), then `copilot login` with an account that has a Copilot seat, and claude-opus-5.5, claude-sonnet-5.5 and gpt-6.1-sol enabled for it |
+
+Then clone the factory and check it works:
+
+```bash
+git clone https://github.com/arananet/anvil-factory.git
+cd anvil-factory
+python3 -m unittest discover -s tests        # about 90 seconds, must end with OK
+python3 tools/model_route_smoke.py           # gateway runtime only: small calls to each model route
+```
+
+The smoke test probes the gateway routes; if it names a route as failing,
+nothing else will work on the default runtime until it passes. It does not
+probe Copilot CLI. With `ANVIL_RUNTIME=copilot`, local runs use your own
+Copilot sign-in and are billed to your own seat.
+
+## 2. Start a project
+
+Each project lives in its own repository.
+
+```bash
+python3 -m anvil.cli new-project ../my-project --name MyProject --blueprint python-service --idea "What this project should prove"
+cd ../my-project
+ln -s ../anvil-factory .anvil-factory
+```
+
+Blueprints: `python-service` for code, `static-site` for pages,
+`cloudflare-worker` for a Worker.
+
+The scaffold gives you the brain seeded with your idea, a first spec to fill
+in, ignore rules that keep secrets out, and a status page. It does not yet tell the project how to reach the factory. Add
+this block to `anvil.yaml` (the file is JSON), between the `"efficiency"`
+block and `"stations"`:
+
+```json
+"runtime": {
+  "kind": "command",
+  "command": "python3 .anvil-factory/tools/dispatch_station.py {packet} {output}",
+  "timeout_seconds": 1800
+},
+```
+
+Then put it on GitHub:
+
+```bash
+git init -b main && git add -A && git commit -m "Scaffold MyProject with the ANVIL software factory"
+gh repo create <owner>/my-project --private --source . --remote origin --push
+```
+
+To bring an existing codebase in instead, run
+`python3 -m anvil.cli onboard <path>`. It reads the project, adds only the
+factory files that are missing and reports the gaps as suggested first work
+orders. It never replaces a file you already have.
+
+## 3. Write a work order
+
+A work order is one unit of work. Save it as `work-orders/<id>.json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "id": "wo-1",
+  "kind": "feature",
+  "title": "Survey score insights",
+  "source": "manual",
+  "description": "Write the spec in .openspec/specs/core-service/spec.md and implement it. Build insights.py with summarize(scores) ... Tests live in tests/test_insights.py and map to the spec scenarios. Keep it minimal.",
+  "risk_hints": [],
+  "status": "new"
+}
+```
+
+- `kind` is one of: `idea`, `feature`, `bug`, `incident`, `task`, `content`.
+- `id` is a short name without slashes; it becomes the folder `runs/<id>/`.
+- A good description says what to build, where it goes, how it is proven, and
+  what is out of scope. Name the spec file when there is one.
+- `risk_hints` may list `auth`, `payments`, `data-deletion`,
+  `production-deploy`, `secrets` or `publishing`. Any of them forces a human
+  decision even when every review passes.
+
+## 4. Pick a pipeline
+
+Pipelines are in the factory's `pipelines/` folder. Start small.
+
+| Pipeline | Stations | Use it for |
+|---|---|---|
+| `scout` | 1 | Read only recon of a codebase, no changes |
+| `docs-only` | 4 | A small change with one review |
+| `plan-build-test` | 6 | Spec, tests, build and a test review. The best first run for a new project |
+| `hotfix` | 7 | An urgent fix with spec and security review |
+| `greenfield` | 13 | A new project from an idea, with the full review set |
+| `sdlc-default` | 15 | The full line for work on an existing project |
+| `content-short` | 10 | A short post |
+| `content-default` | 15 | A full content piece with media |
+
+More stations means more model calls and more tokens. As a reference, one
+`plan-build-test` run for a small function took about nine minutes and
+measured 874,331 tokens through Copilot CLI, which resends its setup context
+on every model turn; that is above the default `max_run_tokens` (see
+section 9).
+
+## 5. Dry run first
+
+Without `--execute` the factory makes no model call. It writes the packet
+each station would receive, so you can read exactly what will be asked.
+
+```bash
+PYTHONPATH=.anvil-factory python3 -m anvil.cli run . --pipeline .anvil-factory/pipelines/plan-build-test.json --work-order work-orders/wo-1.json
+```
+
+Look at `runs/wo-1/*.packet.md`. The report will say `no_reviews`, which is
+expected for a dry run. To see which craft skills each station gets:
+
+```bash
+PYTHONPATH=.anvil-factory python3 -m anvil.cli check-skills . --work-order work-orders/wo-1.json --pipeline .anvil-factory/pipelines/plan-build-test.json
+```
+
+## 6. Run the line
+
+Run on a branch named `anvil/run-<id>`, never on `main`:
+
+```bash
+git checkout -b anvil/run-wo-1
+PYTHONPATH=.anvil-factory python3 -m anvil.cli run . --pipeline .anvil-factory/pipelines/plan-build-test.json --work-order work-orders/wo-1.json --execute
+```
+
+Each station now runs through the selected runtime in your project folder.
+To use Copilot CLI, put `ANVIL_RUNTIME=copilot` in front of the command. Producing
+stations (spec-writer, test-writer, builder and the content producers) may
+edit files. Reviewers can run commands and write only their own review file.
+
+## 7. Read the result
+
+Everything about the run is in `runs/<id>/`:
+
+| File | What it tells you |
+|---|---|
+| `report.md` | The verdict and the reason, in plain words. Read this first |
+| `report.json` | The same, plus models used and tokens per station |
+| `<station>.output.md` | What each station answered |
+| `<station>.review.json` | Each reviewer's verdict and findings |
+| `<station>.packet.md` | What each station was asked |
+
+Verdicts:
+
+| Verdict | Meaning | What you do |
+|---|---|---|
+| `approved` | Every review produced in the run passed | Open a pull request for a human to merge |
+| `changes_requested` | A reviewer wants changes | Read the findings, fix or rerun |
+| `rejected` | A reviewer failed the change | Read the findings; usually rewrite the work order or the spec |
+| `human_required` | Reviews passed but an expected reviewer was not counted | A person decides; check why the review is missing |
+| `gate_failed` | The project's own checks failed before review | Fix what the checks report, rerun |
+| `budget_exceeded` | The run hit its token or call limit | See limits below |
+| `no_reviews` | No review was produced | Normal for a dry run; otherwise check for failed steps |
+
+Intake can also stop a work order before any work: `rejected`,
+`waiting_info` (it needs an answer from you) or `rescoped` (it narrowed the
+request and continued).
+
+Run the project's own tests yourself before you trust an `approved`.
+
+To run again after a fix, use the same command. The previous report is kept
+under `runs/<id>/history/`.
+
+## 8. Hand over to a human
+
+The factory never merges. Commit the change and the run evidence, push the
+branch and open a pull request with the project's template:
+
+```bash
+git add -A && git commit -m "ANVIL run wo-1: <title> (verdict approved)"
+git push -u origin anvil/run-wo-1
+gh pr create --base main --head anvil/run-wo-1 --title "ANVIL run wo-1: <title>"
+```
+
+Someone other than the author reviews and merges. Commit `runs/<id>/` with the
+change; it is the evidence.
+
+## 9. Limits and cost
+
+Each project sets its limits in `anvil.yaml` under `efficiency`:
+
+| Setting | Default | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| `max_run_tokens` | 250,000 | No new station starts once the run has used this much |
+| `max_agent_calls` | 16 | Station calls per run, corrections included |
+| `max_packet_tokens` | 12,000 | An oversized packet stops the run instead of being cut |
+
+A station that is already running can pass the limit; the check happens
+between stations. If a run ends with `budget_exceeded`, either use a smaller
+pipeline or raise `max_run_tokens` for that project.
+
+Copilot CLI counts its resent setup context as cached input on every turn, so
+a project that runs on it needs a higher `max_run_tokens`; 1,500,000 has been
+enough for `plan-build-test`. Copilot bills premium requests, not tokens: the
+token counts measure how much the run consumed, they are not the charge.
+
+## 10. Running in GitHub Actions
+
+Local runs are the simplest path today. Projects are scaffolded without a
+workflow.
+
+In the factory repository, each run workflow is tied to one work order and one
+`anvil/run-*` branch. To run a factory work order in Actions:
+
+1. Copy an existing workflow, for example
+   `.github/workflows/anvil-execute-tier-map.yml`, and change the branch name,
+   the work order path and the issue number.
+2. Push the work order and the workflow to that branch.
+3. Open the run in the Actions tab. The workflow reads the gateway credential
+   from the `CF_AIG_TOKEN` repository secret.
+
+## 11. When something goes wrong
+
+| What you see | Cause | Fix |
+|---|---|---|
+| `--execute needs a runtime object in the project anvil.yaml` | The runtime block is missing | Add the block from step 2 |
+| `the copilot CLI is not on PATH` | Copilot CLI is not installed | `npm install -g @github/copilot` |
+| `Model "..." is not available` | Copilot runtime: the model is not enabled for your account | Enable it in the Copilot policy |
+| `unknown model` or `unknown runtime` | A model or `ANVIL_RUNTIME` value the factory does not know | Use the models in `anvil.yaml`; set `ANVIL_RUNTIME` to `claude-codex` or `copilot`, or unset it |
+| `budget_exceeded` | The run reached its limit | Smaller pipeline, or raise `max_run_tokens` |
+| A reviewer step with `review_file_missing` or `review_parse_failed` | The reviewer did not write a valid review file, even after one correction | Rerun; if it repeats, read the reviewer's output file |
+| `Invalid work order kind` | `kind` is not one of the six allowed values | Fix the work order |
+| The secret scan test fails | A credential-shaped string or a secret file is about to be committed | Remove it and rotate the credential |
+
+## 12. Rules
+
+- Never commit a credential. Use `.env` files locally; they are ignored.
+- Never merge your own run. The human gate is the point.
+- Do not put confidential or personal data in a work order.
+- Keep one work order small enough to review in one sitting.
+- Do not edit the line itself (`anvil/`, `tools/`, `stations/`, `pipelines/`,
+  workflows) without a review.
+
+## Command reference
+
+Run from the factory folder as `python3 -m anvil.cli <command>`, or from a
+project as `PYTHONPATH=.anvil-factory python3 -m anvil.cli <command>`.
+
+| Command | What it does |
+|---|---|
+| `new-project <path> --name N --blueprint B [--idea TEXT]` | Scaffold a new project from a blueprint |
+| `init <path> --name N` | Scaffold a bare project |
+| `onboard <path> [--name N]` | Add the factory files to an existing project |
+| `new-spec <project> <slug> --title T` | Create a spec from the template |
+| `run <project> --pipeline P --work-order W [--execute]` | Run the line; without `--execute` it only writes packets |
+| `validate-pipeline <file>` | Check a pipeline file |
+| `check <project> --slug S` | Check the spec, test and review gates for one spec |
+| `check-skills <project> [--work-order W --pipeline P]` | Validate the skills catalog and show what each station gets |
+| `status-page <project> [--out DIR]` | Regenerate the run status page |
+| `check-brain <project>` | Check the verified learnings store |
+| `supervise <project>` | Propose pipeline changes from past run reports; nothing applies itself |
